{
  "policy_id": "ld-72-compensate-1950s-born-women-waspi",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "Compensate 1950s-Born Women (WASPI)",
  "outcome": "O12",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O12",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "major",
    "time_horizon": "immediate",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Compensating 1950s-born women for pension age changes would cost between \u00a33.5 billion and \u00a310.5 billion at minimum, with no identified funding source \u2014 adding directly to public borrowing or requiring cuts elsewhere. The main uncertainty is the final compensation level and whether any scheme would be funded or deficit-financed.",
    "biggest_unknown": "The compensation level chosen \u2014 ranging from the PHSO's \u00a33.5\u2013\u00a310.5bn to much larger sums \u2014 and whether any payment would be funded through taxation, cuts, or borrowing determines how severely the debt path is affected.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy commits to compensation for 1950s-born women but specifies no funding mechanism or amount.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Ensure that women born in the 1950s are finally treated fairly and properly compensated"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Approximately 3.6 million women are affected by the pension age changes.",
        "evidence_id": "E24",
        "quote": "estimated 3.62 million women born between 6 April 1950 and 5 April 1960 who are affected"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The IFS judged a broader \u00a358 billion compensation pledge would cause public finances to go 'off target'.",
        "evidence_id": "E20",
        "quote": "arguing it would compensate \"relatively well off on average\" individuals and would cause public finances to go \"off target\""
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The current government rejected the PHSO compensation recommendation as neither fair nor feasible and not good value for taxpayers.",
        "evidence_id": "E13",
        "quote": "The current Labour government has rejected the PHSO's recommendation for financial compensation, deeming it \"neither fair nor feasible\" and stating it would not be \"good value for taxpayers\""
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The government explicitly stated a compensation scheme would not be affordable given the state of public finances.",
        "evidence_id": "E30",
        "quote": "The government has explicitly stated that a compensation scheme would not be affordable or good value for taxpayers given the state of public finances"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy commits to compensation but states no funding source, budget envelope, or financing instrument. The minimum credible cost estimate \u2014 using the PHSO's own recommended range \u2014 is \u00a33.5\u2013\u00a310.5 billion for roughly 3.62 million affected women. Campaigners seek significantly more, and a broader framing could push costs much higher. None of the evidence units identify a funding mechanism; the policy text is silent on this. Absent a specified offset \u2014 a tax rise, spending cut, or ring-fenced fund \u2014 a commitment of this scale would be deficit-financed, worsening the near-term debt path. The IFS specifically warned that a \u00a358 billion version would put public finances 'off target', and the government's own assessment called even the PHSO minimum 'not affordable'. On the O12 criteria, this is unfunded spending financing consumption (one-off payments to retirees), not productive investment \u2014 the borrowing-to-invest carve-out that can improve long-run sustainability does not apply here. The effect on the debt path is therefore negative at any plausible compensation level. The magnitude is major because even the floor estimate (\u00a33.5bn) represents a substantial one-off addition to borrowing in a constrained fiscal environment; higher compensation levels would compound this. The PHSO maladministration finding is relevant to O9 (fair treatment) but does not alter the fiscal arithmetic scored here. Confidence is moderate rather than high because the final compensation level is unresolved and a funded scheme (e.g. tax-financed) would reduce \u2014 though not eliminate \u2014 the O12 impact.",
